  "title": "Matrix Arithmetic.",
  "body": " Matrix Arithmetic    Write a function scaleMatrix that takes a matrix A and a scalar k , and returns a new matrix where every element of A has been multiplied by k . Do not use a loop.        Inputs:  A  (mxn) double  matrix to scale     k  (1x1) double  scale factor    Outputs:  B  (mxn) double  scaled matrix      Test Cases ⤵︎  A = [1, 2, 3; 4, 5, 6]; B = scaleMatrix(A, 10) B = scaleMatrix(A, 0) B = scaleMatrix(A, -1)    Expected ⤵︎  B = [10, 20, 30; 40, 50, 60] B = [0, 0, 0; 0, 0, 0] B = [-1, -2, -3; -4, -5, -6]        Write a function shiftMatrix that takes a matrix A and a scalar s , and returns a new matrix where s has been added to every element of A . Do not use a loop.        Inputs:  A  (mxn) double  matrix to shift     s  (1x1) double  amount to add to every element    Outputs:  B  (mxn) double  shifted matrix      Test Cases ⤵︎  A = [1, 2, 3; 4, 5, 6]; B = shiftMatrix(A, 100) B = shiftMatrix(A, -1)    Expected ⤵︎  B = [101, 102, 103; 104, 105, 106] B = [0, 1, 2; 3, 4, 5]        Write a function elementwiseProduct that takes two matrices A and B of the same shape, and returns a new matrix whose elements are the element-by-element products: the entry at position (i, j) of the result is A(i, j) * B(i, j) . Do not use a loop.        Inputs:  A  (mxn) double  first matrix     B  (mxn) double  second matrix, same shape as A    Outputs:  C  (mxn) double  element-wise product of A and B      Test Cases ⤵︎  A = [1, 2; 3, 4]; B = [10, 20; 30, 40]; C = elementwiseProduct(A, B) A = [1, 1, 1; 1, 1, 1]; B = [2, 4, 6; 8, 10, 12]; C = elementwiseProduct(A, B)    Expected ⤵︎  C = [10, 40; 90, 160] C = [2, 4, 6; 8, 10, 12]        Write a function matrixSquare that takes a matrix A and returns a new matrix where every element of A has been squared. Do not use a loop.  Be careful: this is not the same as multiplying the matrix by itself. You want each individual entry squared.        Inputs:  A  (mxn) double  matrix to square element-wise    Outputs:  B  (mxn) double  matrix where each element is the square of the corresponding element of A      Test Cases ⤵︎  A = [1, 2, 3; 4, 5, 6]; B = matrixSquare(A) A = [-2, 0, 3; 1, -4, 5]; B = matrixSquare(A)    Expected ⤵︎  B = [ 1, 4, 9; 16, 25, 36] B = [4, 0, 9; 1, 16, 25]        Write a function absDifference that takes two matrices A and B of the same shape, and returns a new matrix containing the absolute value of the element-wise difference between them: the entry at position (i, j) of the result is . Do not use a loop.        Inputs:  A  (mxn) double  first matrix     B  (mxn) double  second matrix, same shape as A    Outputs:  D  (mxn) double  element-wise absolute differences      Test Cases ⤵︎  A = [1, 5; 9, 2]; B = [3, 2; 4, 8]; D = absDifference(A, B) A = [10, 20, 30]; B = [15, 18, 30]; D = absDifference(A, B)    Expected ⤵︎  D = [2, 3; 5, 6] D = [5, 2, 0]        Write a function normalizeByMax that takes a matrix A and returns a new matrix where every element has been divided by the largest element of A . After this transformation, the largest element of the result should be exactly 1 , and every other element should be a fraction between 0 and 1 (assuming all entries of A are non-negative).  To find the largest element of an entire matrix, you need a way to ask max to look at every entry, not just one row or column at a time. The standard MATLAB idiom for this is to write A(:) , which flattens the matrix into a single long column vector containing every value. Then max(A(:)) returns the largest element of the whole matrix.  Do not use a loop.        Inputs:  A  (mxn) double  matrix with at least one non-zero element    Outputs:  B  (mxn) double  matrix scaled so its largest element is 1      Test Cases ⤵︎  A = [1, 2; 4, 8]; B = normalizeByMax(A) A = [10, 20, 30; 5, 25, 15]; B = normalizeByMax(A)    Expected ⤵︎  B = [0.125, 0.25; 0.5, 1.0] B ≈ [0.333, 0.667, 1.000; 0.167, 0.833, 0.500]        When a matrix represents data with a known upper bound — for example, pixel brightnesses on a 0-to-255 scale — it is often useful to normalize the data by dividing every entry by that upper bound. The result is a matrix whose values lie between 0 and 1.  Write a function normalizeBy that takes a matrix A and a positive scalar m , and returns a new matrix where every element of A has been divided by m . Do not use a loop.        Inputs:  A  (mxn) double  matrix to normalize     m  (1x1) double  positive normalization value    Outputs:  B  (mxn) double  normalized matrix      Test Cases ⤵︎  A = [1, 2; 4, 8]; B = normalizeBy(A, 8) A = [128, 64, 255; 0, 192, 255]; B = normalizeBy(A, 255)    Expected ⤵︎  B = [0.125, 0.25; 0.5, 1.0] B ≈ [0.502, 0.251, 1.000; 0, 0.753, 1.000]      "
